WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Jason!

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.270
What's the weather
like in London?

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.700
JASON BERRYMAN: Well,
Martin, it's the UK,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.117
so of course it's
raining outside.

00:00:08.117 --> 00:00:08.700
How about you?

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.268
How's your weather
in California?

00:00:10.268 --> 00:00:12.810
MARTIN OMANDER: You know, I've
had the curtains drawn all day

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.320
because I'm recording videos,
so I really don't know.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.180
But today, we're
talking about how

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.130
to coordinate microservices.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:23.670
Is it better to have a central
coordinator-- orchestration--

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.230
or is it better
for control to be

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.710
distributed among the
services-- choreography?

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.960
JASON BERRYMAN: Well, with
so many things in life,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:31.890
it depends.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.810
I'd be happy to share
what I've learned

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.085
from a recent large project.

00:00:35.085 --> 00:00:38.286
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:44.745 --> 00:00:47.120
MARTIN OMANDER: Thanks for
joining me on the show, Jason.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.533
You're a freelance Google
Cloud architect at 418.dev?

00:00:50.533 --> 00:00:51.950
JASON BERRYMAN:
Yes, that's right.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.700
I'm also a Google Developers
expert for both Firebase

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.240
and Google Cloud platform,
and I run the Google Developer

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.455
group in Milton Keynes,
which is just outside London.

00:01:03.455 --> 00:01:04.330
MARTIN OMANDER: Nice.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.610
I love Google Developer groups.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.860
They're great places for
meeting other developers

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.090
and learning from each other.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:13.480
Now, you're helping your
parking company in the UK

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.787
build their system on top of
Google Cloud platform, right?

00:01:16.787 --> 00:01:17.620
JASON BERRYMAN: Yes.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.430
The company collects vehicle
movement and parking permit

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:24.478
data from car parks
and motorway services.

00:01:24.478 --> 00:01:25.270
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.580
And what is that data used for?

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.980
JASON BERRYMAN: They use the
data with serverless Google

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.310
Cloud products to provide
analytics and metrics

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:34.630
to their customers.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.933
The data can also be used
for parking enforcement.

00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:39.850
MARTIN OMANDER: Ooh, you
mentioned serverless.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.440
I love that stuff.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.223
Why did they pick serverless?

00:01:43.223 --> 00:01:44.890
JASON BERRYMAN: They're
using serverless

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:46.930
so that they can focus
their engineering

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.270
hours on new
feature development,

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:54.642
and to minimize the operations
work for their running systems.

00:01:54.642 --> 00:01:55.600
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.420
Now there are two ways of
coordinating microservices.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.520
Orchestration-- a
central coordinator

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.070
calls all the microservices
in the right order,

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:06.370
or choreography--
microservices send and receive

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.949
events and act on them,
so control is distributed.

00:02:09.949 --> 00:02:12.730
Which one did you pick for
the parking company, Jason?

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.520
JASON BERRYMAN: Well, each
one has particular strengths

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:16.240
and weaknesses.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.845
So we used both.

00:02:17.845 --> 00:02:18.700
MARTIN OMANDER: Hmm.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:19.963
Tell me more about that.

00:02:19.963 --> 00:02:21.380
JASON BERRYMAN:
Well, for example,

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:24.490
when a car enters a
parking lot, a camera

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.890
takes a picture of
their license plate.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.290
The picture of the
vehicle going in

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.380
gets matched with the picture
of the vehicle going out.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:36.010
And that matched visit then gets
linked to one or more parking

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:36.670
permits.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.197
MARTIN OMANDER: Hmm, that
sounds kind of complicated.

00:02:39.197 --> 00:02:40.030
JASON BERRYMAN: Yes.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:44.440
And to make it simpler, we have
multiple services, each one

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.220
doing part of the work.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.850
So for example, we've
got one service which

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.770
ingests the data
from the cameras

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:54.070
and passes it through the
visit-matching service,

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.460
then another one which
ingests the permit data

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.232
and matches it with the visits.

00:02:59.232 --> 00:03:00.190
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.110
Now, how do you
coordinate these services?

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:04.900
I imagine the camera
ingestion service

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.410
needs to let the matching
service know that a vehicle has

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:09.910
entered or exited?

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:11.770
JASON BERRYMAN: Well,
we use orchestration,

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:15.910
where a central controller
calls a series of services

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.088
in a pipeline and
returns a result.

00:03:18.088 --> 00:03:18.880
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.810
That sounds pretty
straightforward.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.720
It's nice to have control of
the business logic in one place,

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:24.220
right?

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.053
JASON BERRYMAN: Yes.

00:03:25.053 --> 00:03:27.670
But sometimes,
that isn't optimal.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.430
We are using a NoSQL
document database

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.620
that is optimized for reads.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.680
So we can sometimes
need to normalize data.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.870
For example, an administrator
may update the expiry date

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.690
for a whole group
of parking permits.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:46.780
If the expiry date
has been extended,

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:50.170
a visit that was
beyond the expiry date

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:52.430
may now be within it.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:56.830
So the driver shouldn't have to
pay a parking penalty charge.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:59.450
MARTIN OMANDER: Everyone hates
paying parking penalties,

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:02.380
especially if their
permit was just extended.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.150
Now, how do you prevent that?

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.370
JASON BERRYMAN: When the
permit group is updated,

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:10.360
we trigger updates of all
the permits in that group.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.220
This, in turn,
means that we have

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:15.178
to match the permits
against the visits again.

00:04:15.178 --> 00:04:15.970
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.972
But why can't you use a
central coordinator for this?

00:04:18.972 --> 00:04:20.680
JASON BERRYMAN: If we
only want to change

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.590
the expiry date
of one permit, we

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.260
don't need to update
any other permits--

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:30.560
we only need to trigger
the visit-matching again.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.190
A central coordinator
would become very complex

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.270
if it should be able to handle
both updates to many permits

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:40.820
and updates just to
individual permits.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.050
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, I see.

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:44.090
So if you don't
use orchestration,

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:45.500
what do you use instead?

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.290
JASON BERRYMAN: Then we use
the other method, choreography,

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:51.290
where each service
receives messages,

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.290
takes action on those
messages, and sends

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:56.828
new messages to
the other services.

00:04:56.828 --> 00:04:58.370
MARTIN OMANDER: So
in what situations

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.380
do you pick orchestration,
and when do you

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.300
pick choreography instead?

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.880
JASON BERRYMAN: When the
process is always the same,

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:06.860
orchestration shines.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.980
For example, when a
car enters, we always

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:12.380
save the raw camera data.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.890
The data is transformed from
each camera's manufactured,

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:19.970
proprietary format to
our standard data format.

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:22.970
And then we extract
the images from that

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:26.710
and save them into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:31.630
Finally, we send the event data
to Cloud Pub/Sub for processing

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:32.995
further down the pipeline.

00:05:32.995 --> 00:05:34.370
MARTIN OMANDER:
That makes sense.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.458
And when do you
use choreography?

00:05:36.458 --> 00:05:38.000
JASON BERRYMAN: When
we have services

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.240
which may form
part of a workflow,

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.390
but may also be
triggered individually.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:45.980
A good example is the
permit group scenario

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:47.202
we talked about earlier.

00:05:47.202 --> 00:05:48.160
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.960
Now, it sounds like
choreography is

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.300
based on having many triggers.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:55.390
Is it messy to have lots of
triggers all over the system?

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:57.460
How do you manage
that complexity?

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.280
JASON BERRYMAN: If you're using
Cloud Functions or Cloud Run

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.710
services directly
in Google Cloud,

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:06.220
it's really essential to
maintain good documentation

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:09.250
and use a tool like Deployment
Manager or Terraform

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:10.760
to deploy them.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.780
However, if you're using
the Firebase suite,

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:17.440
it's much easier to
develop, test, and deploy

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:18.610
your Cloud Functions.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:20.908
There's even a great
local emulator.

00:06:20.908 --> 00:06:21.700
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.490
Does that mean that you
prefer to use Cloud Functions

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.015
for Firebase all the time?

00:06:26.015 --> 00:06:28.390
JASON BERRYMAN: Cloud Functions
for Firebase only support

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:32.690
Node.js and TypeScript, which
is great for web developers,

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:34.870
but if your team uses
another language--

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.820
such as Go or Python--

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.832
it may not be such a great fit.

00:06:38.832 --> 00:06:39.790
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:42.460
And which Google
products do you recommend

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:45.970
for orchestration and for
choreography, respectively?

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:48.490
JASON BERRYMAN: For
orchestration, Workflows

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:52.090
is a great product for designing
orchestration pipelines.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:54.850
Workflows can be triggered
by other services,

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:58.990
such as Cloud Functions,
Cloud Run, or Cloud Scheduler.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:02.260
For choreography,
you can use Eventarc

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:06.370
to trigger individual Cloud
Run or Cloud Function services,

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.140
based on individual events.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:10.930
However, there
are some services,

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.840
like Cloud Firestore
and Cloud Storage,

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.360
which can trigger Cloud
Functions directly

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.228
without Eventarc,
whenever an event happens.

00:07:19.228 --> 00:07:20.020
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.090
And to recap, when
would you recommend

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:25.990
using orchestration versus
a choreography architecture?

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:27.490
JASON BERRYMAN: If
you're developing

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:30.970
a traditional n-tier
system on Google Cloud,

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:34.580
it's probably best to use
the orchestration model.

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:36.730
However, if you're
building a user

00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:39.010
interface-focused
application with something

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:43.270
like the Firebase suite, this is
where choreography works really

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:44.240
well.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.840
However, there may
be times that you

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:49.580
need to create a hybrid
app, in which case,

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:52.132
you can leverage the
best of both worlds.

00:07:52.132 --> 00:07:53.840
MARTIN OMANDER: The
best of both worlds--

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:55.310
I like that.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:56.810
Thank you for joining
me and sharing

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:58.940
your real-world
experience, Jason.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:00.270
JASON BERRYMAN: Happy to do it.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:02.520
MARTIN OMANDER: And thank
you, everyone, for watching.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.700
If you have any questions
for Jason or me,

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.290
add a comment below.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:09.020
Also, let me know if there are
other serverless topics you'd

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:10.850
like to see in future episodes.

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:12.020
Bye for now.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:15.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

